## The angle: the second price

A DTC brand's proof lives on a website. A shelf has a front panel and four seconds. The day you land the retail account you don't add a channel — you publish a **second, cheaper price for the identical product**, and the customer most likely to find it is the one who believed you first.

The evidence is entirely brand-owned and checkable in ten minutes, which is what made it win:

| | |
|---|---|
| Ritual at Costco | $48.99 / 60 days = **82¢ a day** — their words: *"our lowest cost per day available anywhere it's sold,"* *"the exact same formula sold on Ritual.com"* |
| Ritual subscription | *"Regular price $39.00"* / 30 days = **$1.30 a day** |
| The subscriber pays | **~59% more, ~$174/year** |

Nothing went wrong — club economics force that math on every brand entering the door. The gap is that **no page anywhere tells the subscriber what she's still buying.** That's a message decision, and it's exactly the Angle Map ("retail push" is already a named live occasion in the canon).

Counter-case in the same month: Applied Nutrition licensed Sour Patch Kids and Swedish Fish from Mondelēz into ~2,200 Walmart and ~1,300 GNC doors — skipping the compression problem by borrowing belief instead of building it. Two brands, opposite answers, one unwritten question.

